Comment (internal/service/relational/templates/subject_template_service.go:1344):
**Low — `component-type` on a template that isn't `component` is silently accepted and stored.** A `resource` (or `user`, `party`, …) template sent with `component-type: software` passes validation and is persisted. That value is never used: these templates produce no subjects, and the resolver in API-06 only reads `ComponentType` for component templates. A plugin author gets no signal that the field was ignored. The warning at L1694 only mentions the type.

Suggestion, either one:
- reject it: `if payload.ComponentType != nil && payload.Type != subjectTemplateTypeComponent { return newValidationError("componentType is only valid for component templates") }`, or
- keep accepting it and extend the non-component warning (L1694) to mention that `component-type` is ignored, e.g. `template <name> has type <type> and will not produce subjects (component-type ignored)`.

Either way, add a test for the chosen behaviour.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Fix (option 2, keep accepting): the design already accepts non-component templates and warns about them, so rejecting them would widen what counts as a hard failure. In BatchUpsert's warning loop, when a non-component item has ComponentType != nil, append ' (component-type ignored)' to the existing spec message. Templates without component-type keep the exact spec text. Add a service unit test and extend the handler integration test for this case.

